---
import {
  ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS,
  ACCOUNT_TOOL_NAMES,
  AGENT_PROMPT_NAME,
  AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL,
  TOOL_DEFINITIONS,
  TOOL_NAMES,
  toolFields,
} from "@exeora/protocol";
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";

/**
 * The tool reference, generated from the contract rather than written beside it.
 *
 * `TOOL_DEFINITIONS` is what the gateway advertises to the client and what the
 * executor validates arguments against. Reading it here at build time is what
 * stops this page from being the third description of the same thing, and the
 * one that quietly goes out of date. Adding a tool publishes its reference.
 */

const tools = TOOL_NAMES.map((name) => ({
  name,
  title: TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].title,
  description: TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].description,
  readOnly: TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].readOnly,
  fields: toolFields(name),
}));

const accountTools = ACCOUNT_TOOL_NAMES.map((name) => ({
  name,
  title: ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].title,
  description: ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].description,
  readOnly: ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].readOnly,
  fields: toolFields(name),
}));
---

<Doc
  title="Tools"
  description="What an agent connected to a project can do, and exactly what each tool takes."
  canonicalPath="/docs/tools/"
>
  <p>
    Project paths are relative to the project root, or to the working copy of the workspace the
    call names, and confined to it before anything touches the disk. <code>read_file</code>, <code>list_files</code>, <code>grep</code> and command{" "}
    <code>cwd</code> may also use <code>~/.agents/AGENTS.md</code> and paths under{" "}
    <code>~/.agents/skills/</code>. Writes cannot. Any other absolute path, or one that climbs out
    with <code>..</code>, is refused with <code>PATH_ESCAPE</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Which of these a project actually offers is up to its policy: see{" "}
    <a href="/docs/policy/">what a project allows</a>. Tools marked <strong>read only</strong> change
    nothing, which is what a project set to read only is left with, and what is never interrupted by
    a confirmation prompt.
  </p>

  <p>
    This page is generated from the same definitions the gateway advertises and the executor
    validates against, so it cannot describe a tool that does not exist or miss an argument that
    does.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the account URL, <code>https://exeora.dev/mcp</code>, every tool below also takes an optional{" "}
    <code>project</code>, naming the project to run that one call without changing shared state.
    A project's own URL has no such argument: it carries its project in the path, and offering a way
    to name another is exactly what it does not do.
  </p>

  <p>
    File, command and process tools accept <code>workspace</code>, naming a connected workspace by
    slug or id, and run on the machine that holds it. Omit it or pass <code>main</code> for the
    project root at the default <a href="/docs/projects/">location</a>.{" "}
    <code>create_workspace</code> accepts it as an optional source, while{" "}
    <code>detach_workspace</code> and <code>remove_workspace</code> require it as their target.{" "}
    <code>list_git_workspaces</code> and <code>attach_workspace</code> do not take it. The gateway
    resolves selectors to stable ids and removes the routing field before executor validation. A
    workspace error never falls back to main.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>create_workspace</code> and <code>list_git_workspaces</code> also take{" "}
    <code>where</code>, which names a location of the project: a machine's name, or{" "}
    <code>cloud</code>, as <code>list_projects</code> and <code>list_workspaces</code> name them.
    Without it a workspace is made at the default location, or beside the workspace named by{" "}
    <code>workspace</code>, which is the checkout it starts from. <code>where</code> is read by the
    gateway and never sent to a machine, which by then is the one that was chosen. The first
    workspace on a machine that holds no copy of the repository clones it there first, and one on
    Exeora Cloud starts an instance, so the call can take a minute or more.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>list_workspaces</code> is the gateway's inventory across every location. It lists the
    project root as <code>main</code> and every other workspace with the location that holds it,
    and for a workspace on Exeora Cloud the status of its instance. It works while machines are
    offline. <code>list_git_workspaces</code> asks one machine, requires it to be connected, and
    includes Git checkouts that are not connected to Exeora.
  </p>

  {
    tools.map((tool) => (
      <section>
        <h2 id={tool.name}>
          <code>{tool.name}</code>
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>{tool.title}</strong>
          {tool.readOnly && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · read only</span>}
        </p>

        <p>{tool.description}</p>

        {tool.fields.length > 0 && (
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Argument</th>
                  <th>Type</th>
                  <th>What it means</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {tool.fields.map((field) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.name}</code>
                      {!field.required && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> optional</span>}
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.type}</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>{field.description}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        )}
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="choosing-a-project">Choosing a project</h2>

  <p>
    Two tools are answered by the gateway and never reach a machine, so they work with every
    machine offline and no project policy applies to them. <code>list_projects</code> exists only
    on the account URL, where one connection covers several projects and a caller needs to see the
    valid targets. It shows each project with its repository and the locations it lives in, by the
    names <code>where</code> takes, and the state of each. <code>list_workspaces</code> is on both
    URLs. On a project's own URL it takes no <code>project</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    A repository that <a href="/docs/projects/#nowhere">lives nowhere</a> is listed too, with no locations and <code>machine</code> set to <code>nowhere</code>. Nothing can serve a call to it until it has a location again. <code>create_workspace</code> with <code>where</code> set to <code>cloud</code> gives it one. Exeora Cloud in state <code>no instance</code> is the default location with no instance for the project root: the next call to the project root makes it and is answered with <code>EXECUTOR_WAKING</code>, to be sent again in about a minute.
  </p>

  <p>
    With one project granted, there is nothing to choose and calls may omit <code>project</code>. With
    more, every executor tool call must carry a project slug or id. The target belongs to that call,
    so two conversations in the same client can work in different projects without moving each
    other.
  </p>

  {
    accountTools.map((tool) => (
      <section>
        <h2 id={tool.name}>
          <code>{tool.name}</code>
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>{tool.title}</strong>
          {tool.readOnly && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · read only</span>}
          {tool.name === "list_projects" && (
            <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · account URL only</span>
          )}
        </p>

        <p>{tool.description}</p>

        {tool.fields.length > 0 && (
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Argument</th>
                  <th>Type</th>
                  <th>What it means</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {tool.fields.map((field) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.name}</code>
                      {!field.required && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> optional</span>}
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.type}</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>{field.description}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        )}
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="reading-the-manual">Reading the manual</h2>

  <p>
    One more, on both URLs, taking no arguments and reaching no machine.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2 id={AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.name}>
      <code>{AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.name}</code>
    </h2>

    <p>
      <strong>{AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.title}</strong>
      <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · read only</span>
    </p>

    <p>{AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.description}</p>

    <p>
      It returns a constant from the gateway, so it answers with the machine asleep and no project
      policy applies to it. The same text arrives as the <code>{AGENT_PROMPT_NAME}</code> prompt, and
      a shorter form of it in the handshake. See <a href="/docs/agent-prompt/">the agent prompt</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
